# Events Module

## Purpose

Maps raw SDL keyboard events to semantic game event IDs and dispatches them to registered observers. Decouples game logic from SDL input handling.

## Key Classes

| Class | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `InputController` | Maintains a `SDL_Keycode -> event_id` map; on `Handle()`, dispatches press/release to subscribed `Observer`s |
| `InputController::Observer` | Interface: `OnInputEvent( event_id, on )` -- `on=true` for key down, `on=false` for key up |

## API

```cpp
// Define key-to-event-ID bindings
std::unordered_map<SDL_Keycode, uint64_t> bindings =
{
    { SDLK_w,     GameEvents::THRUST    },
    { SDLK_a,     GameEvents::TURN_LEFT },
    { SDLK_SPACE, GameEvents::FIRE      }
};
InputController input( bindings );

// Register observer (typically in game scene init)
input.Subscribe( GameEvents::THRUST, &my_observer );
input.Subscribe( GameEvents::FIRE,   &my_observer );

// Feed SDL events from main loop
SDL_Event event;
while( SDL_PollEvent( &event ) )
{
    input.Handle( event );
}

// Observer implementation
class PlayerSystem : public InputController::Observer
{
    void OnInputEvent( uint64_t event_id, bool on ) override
    {
        if( event_id == GameEvents::THRUST ) mThrusting = on;
        if( event_id == GameEvents::FIRE && on ) QueueShot();
    }
};
```

## Key Patterns

- **Event ID is a plain `uint64_t`:** Define event IDs as enum or constexpr values in the game layer. The module has no knowledge of game-specific events.
- **Per-event subscription:** `Subscribe( event_id, observer )` registers one observer for one event ID. The same observer can subscribe to multiple events.
- **Press and release:** `on=true` / `on=false` carries both key-down and key-up. Observers must handle both if they care about sustained input (e.g., held thrust).
- **Unmapped keys are silently ignored:** Only keys present in the binding map are dispatched.

## DOs

- Define all event IDs as `constexpr uint64_t` or an `enum class` in the game layer (e.g., `GameConfig.h`)
- Subscribe per-event rather than handling all events in one observer -- keeps logic separated
- Call `input.Handle()` once per SDL event in the main loop -- before `Update()` calls

## DON'Ts

- Don't poll `SDL_GetKeyboardState()` directly in game code -- route all input through `InputController`
- Don't reuse event IDs for different actions -- collisions cause incorrect dispatch
- Don't remove observers mid-dispatch (inside `OnInputEvent`) -- undefined behaviour
